WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.480
Strategy is an important part of a&nbsp;
Formula 1 race and lord knows I bang

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.240
on about it enough so I started thinking&nbsp;
about visual ways of understanding race

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.000
strategies that might be worth sharing.
So that’s what I’m going to do here. And

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.720
to do so, I’m going to really simplify things&nbsp;
down because we don’t need to rush straight

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.880
into 60 lap races with complicated statistical&nbsp;
models of tyre behaviour and all that. We can

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.840
pare that all down and use little squares.
And when I say start simple, I mean it. This

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.000
is a model, so let’s imagine a ten lap,&nbsp;
dry race. Like F1, to complete this race

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.600
you have to use two compounds of tyres&nbsp;
and we’re keeping it simple so let’s

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.120
only consider two compounds: soft and medium.
We’re even going to ignore knowing the exact

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.840
time of things in seconds. Instead, let’s&nbsp;
say that every lap the soft tyre takes

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.920
three units of time. Each block of time we&nbsp;
can represent with a little red block here.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.880
So, lap one, take 3 units of time. After 5 laps&nbsp;
on softs, it’s taken 15 units of time and so on.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.400
The medium, let’s say, uses 4&nbsp;
blocks of time per lap. So it’s

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.440
slower than the soft tyre by one block every lap.
Given just that information and knowing we have to

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:11.280
use both tyres we can reasonably say for a ten lap&nbsp;
race, the fastest strategy is going nine laps on

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.120
the soft tyres and one lap on the slower mediums.
So your total time for the race is 27 units on

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:23.520
softs plus 4 units on mediums, making 31 time&nbsp;
units, plus however long it takes to make your

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.080
pit stop, which we’ll worry about in a moment.
Now let’s add an extra wrinkle. Let’s say the

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.960
soft tyres only last 4 laps. Don’t worry about the&nbsp;
complexities of tyre wear just yet – in this model

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:38.800
the soft tyres take 3 time-blocks per lap every&nbsp;
lap, but after 4 laps they just die, so you can’t

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.360
use them for more than 4 laps. Mediums, let’s&nbsp;
just say last all ten laps. Keeping it simple.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.360
So you can run on soft tyres for 4 laps,&nbsp;
make a pitstop and then run mediums for

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.640
6 laps. That means your first 4 laps on softs&nbsp;
take a time of 12, your 6 laps on the mediums

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:59.040
take a time of 24, giving a total race time&nbsp;
of 36 plus however long a pit stop takes.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.680
If you went a bit shorter on the softs – say only&nbsp;
three laps – how would that effect the race time?

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.960
Well the soft stint would take 9 blocks of time&nbsp;
and the medium stint would take 28 blocks or time,

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.160
totally 37 time units, plus your&nbsp;
pitstop. So a little bit longer.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.960
Hopefully, even though this is&nbsp;
really pared down right now you can

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:22.240
see the basic structure of building a strategy.
OK, so let’s go one step further. We know those

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.520
softs are significantly faster. If we COULD run&nbsp;
them for 9 laps, we know that’s 5 blocks of time

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.920
faster than the fastest one stop. But we can’t,&nbsp;
because they die. But we could spend 8 laps on

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.990
softs if we did two pitstops instead of one…
Let’s map it out. 4 laps on softs, pitstop,

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:44.320
another 4 laps on softs, pitstop, then&nbsp;
finish the race with two laps on mediums.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.400
In lap time alone, that’s a total&nbsp;
of 12 + 12 + 8 blocks of time,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:54.720
coming to 32 for the whole race but now you’ve&nbsp;
got to consider TWO pitstops, not just one.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.400
So is this two stop faster than this&nbsp;
one stop? It depends on how long a

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.480
pitstop takes. And we can do a bit of quick&nbsp;
algebra to figure out the crossover point.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:08.160
The one-stop takes 36 plus a pitstop, the&nbsp;
two-stop takes 32 plus two pitstops so if

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.800
they were equal to each other in time we can just&nbsp;
take 32 off both sides, take a pitstop off both

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:19.360
sides and see clearly that both strategies take&nbsp;
the same time if a pitstop takes 4 units of time.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.720
If a pitstop is longer than&nbsp;
4, a one-stop is quicker. If

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.360
it’s shorter than 4, the two-stop is quicker.
Now, I’m going to reiterate this is just a tiny

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.280
simplified model which is why the numbers&nbsp;
are so exaggerated and we’re asking if a

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.200
pitstop takes as long as an entire lap time.
But to choose which strategy to implement in

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.000
the race, you need to know how&nbsp;
long a pitstop will take you.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.480
The time it will cost you to make a pitstop&nbsp;
is called the ‘pit delta’ as delta is the

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.000
greek letter used in equations to denote&nbsp;
‘difference’. You’ll hear the word ‘delta’

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.120
a lot in F1 speak when talking about differences&nbsp;
in values like speed or time or what have you.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.480
So the pit delta is calculated by taking the&nbsp;
total time spent within the entire pit road

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.360
and subtracting the entire time spent driving&nbsp;
the normal track. And you’ll probably take this

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:05.520
from pit entry to probably the first braking&nbsp;
point after rejoining as that’s when your speed

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.080
likely first re-matches a normal racing lap.
This varies from track to track, of course

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.680
with pitlane speeds, track speeds and how many&nbsp;
corners the pit road cuts. To give some examples:

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.840
Monza is quite simple in that the entire&nbsp;
pitlane in-and-out is down the main straight,

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.120
but it’s a very fast straight. I timed race speed&nbsp;
cars going from here to here in around 16 seconds,

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.480
but through the pits, with a stop, it&nbsp;
takes around 38 seconds. So that’s a

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.600
delta of around 22 seconds – quite large.
Monaco, however, is a slow speed circuit

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.800
and the entry and exit slightly cut&nbsp;
the first and last corners. Here,

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.760
going at racing speed on track takes 12 and a half&nbsp;
seconds, where taking a pitstop takes 32 seconds,

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.680
leaving a delta of about 19.5 seconds.
Silverstone is one of the weirder ones.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.360
The entry actually cuts a large section at&nbsp;
the end of the circuit but the speed limit

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.320
is pretty low to compensate. There’s&nbsp;
a 23 second delta here by my watch.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.640
I should say I did these times watching footage&nbsp;
with a stopwatch so they’re not precise, I’m just

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:02.800
giving examples of variation strategists have to&nbsp;
account for. And they will measure the difference

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:08.320
over an entire lap, not just this section.
Let’s head back to our little one-stop strategy

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.080
again and ask ourselves a question.&nbsp;
Is there any reason to do soft first,

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:17.120
then medium. Or can we do medium, then soft?
Well there are benefits and drawbacks to both.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:21.920
One obvious benefit is softer tyres tend to be&nbsp;
grippy and racy immediately so will help you

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.720
gain or defend position off the grid. Claiming&nbsp;
track position early on is really important as

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.800
overtaking is not necessarily guaranteed so&nbsp;
the launch off the start and into the first

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.680
lap might well set up the rest of your race.
The problem that you might find strapping softs

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.360
on up front is that they’ll wear out more&nbsp;
quickly with a heavier car. Now we haven’t

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.360
factored fuel into this model yet but for&nbsp;
now let’s just consider that fuel is heavy

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.280
and will get used up over the course of the race.
This means that the car is heaviest at the start

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.680
of the race and will require more work from&nbsp;
the tyres. More work means the softs will wear

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.400
out faster, so will not last as long as if you&nbsp;
used them at the end when the car is lighter.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:02.560
So maybe using softs first will only give you 3&nbsp;
laps in our 10- lap model, whereas going mediums

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.800
first will give you 4 laps of softs if used&nbsp;
at the end. You just have to account for the

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.360
fact that you might lose places off the line.
But, okay, let’s talk about tyre degradation.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:14.560
We haven’t really factored it into our model&nbsp;
yet – I just told you the soft tyres died

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.040
after 4 laps. Let’s go one step further.
Tyre degradation can be affected by many

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.760
things – track temperature, the&nbsp;
abrasiveness of the track surface,

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.960
how many fast corners there are – stuff like that.
For our purposes here, let’s keep things simple,

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.120
but increase our model race to 20 laps to&nbsp;
see some of the effects we’re going to add.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.840
Let’s say the soft tyre is at normal strength&nbsp;
for 3 laps, so each lap is 3 blocks of time.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:41.520
But after that each lap gets 1 block longer&nbsp;
for every two laps. So the behaviour will look

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.560
something like this. See how on lap 10, the&nbsp;
lap now takes 7 blocks of time, way more than

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:51.120
the 3-block lap times when the tyre was fresh.
For mediums, let’s say they stay strong for 5

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.560
laps, then gain a block of time every 3 further&nbsp;
laps they go, so their tyre degradation pattern

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:59.440
looks like this lap-on-lap.
So compared to the softs,

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.560
the mediums start off slower but last longer and&nbsp;
take longer to degrade. This means by lap 10,

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.680
the mediums are actually the faster tyre,&nbsp;
taking 6 blocks of time to the softs’ 7.

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:13.440
Let’s say we want to look at potential one-stop&nbsp;
strategies again over a 20 lap race with this

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.440
knowledge of how the compounds degrade. It doesn’t&nbsp;
matter what order we do the stints in for this and

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:22.400
say softs go first before switching to mediums.
This line here marks where we take out pitstop,

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:26.640
so we can see our soft stint on the left and&nbsp;
the total time it takes us and the medium stint

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.120
on the right and the total time that takes. We&nbsp;
can see the changes as we move back and forth.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:35.840
So if we did 4 laps on the softs and 16 on the&nbsp;
mediums, the soft stint takes 13 blocks of time,

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:41.440
the medium stint takes 90 blocks on&nbsp;
time giving a race time total of 103.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.160
And from playing with this model we can see the&nbsp;
ideal strategy here is 9 laps on the soft and 11

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:52.240
laps on the medium, minimising our total time to&nbsp;
just 93 time units. Plus the pitstop, of course.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:55.520
This is great! We made a little model, we&nbsp;
adjusted it to find the minimum overall

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:01.200
race time and now this will be our strategy. Maybe&nbsp;
we’ll even call it something fruity like “Plan A”

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.680
But is this the best we can do? We don’t&nbsp;
have to do just ONE pitstop. What about two

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:10.720
pitstops? Now we can spend more time on the&nbsp;
softs before they collapse. Is this better?

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:13.840
While there were 19 ways of doing a&nbsp;
one-stop, there are 90 different ways

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:17.760
of doing a soft-soft-medium two-stop over 20&nbsp;
laps so I’m not going to go through them all

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.920
here but using the tyre degradation patterns&nbsp;
we’ve laid out there are actually four ways

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.280
to get a minimum two-stop time,&nbsp;
which I’ll just display for you,

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:28.160
rather than read them all out.
Remember, the big numbers are

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:33.280
how much time each stint takes. This&nbsp;
here shows how many laps each stint is.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:38.960
Each of them takes just 79 blocks of time in total&nbsp;
but this time you have to add TWO pitstops in,

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.480
so again it will depend on the pit delta&nbsp;
whether a two stop is better than a one.

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.920
And once again, with a little bit of simple&nbsp;
algebra – same as before – we can say that

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.400
a two stop is quicker in this model if&nbsp;
the pit delta is under 13 blocks of time.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:54.080
I stress, this is a super simplified model&nbsp;
but this is the kind of things that’s going on

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:58.240
when calculating a race strategy but with more&nbsp;
complex models of tyre wear and all the things

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:02.000
we’re talking about layered on each other,&nbsp;
so in real life they’ll be comparing a huge

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.160
number of scenarios against each other, not&nbsp;
just the 90 I did with my little blocks here.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:10.000
But let’s get rid of tyre wear again and think&nbsp;
about fuel again for a second. Because this is

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.640
something you have to layer into your times.
The car starts full of fuel, right? You can’t

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.720
refuel so it’s starting with about 70 kg&nbsp;
of fuel which depletes to almost nothing

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.880
over the course of the race. This means the car&nbsp;
is a bit slower by weight alone at the start,

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:28.080
and gets faster and more nimble as time goes on.
This will affect the pure lap times on each tyre,

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.320
but also the driveability of the car, which is&nbsp;
absolutely something I can’t model with these

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.720
little blocks but does come into determining which&nbsp;
tyre might be best for which phase of the race.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.600
But if we were going to factor in the&nbsp;
weight-saving benefits of using up fuel

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.520
into our times we might add a little diagonal&nbsp;
snip like this or something to show the car

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:50.080
potentially becoming a little faster. Again, the&nbsp;
real models are much more data driven and complex.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:53.760
Strategists will also likely underfuel the&nbsp;
car a little bit for these weight-gaining

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:57.760
time saves and ask the driver to do some&nbsp;
nursing mid-race to balance this out.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:01.520
So, okay, we’ve made these lovely simplified&nbsp;
models and worked out the fastest way to do

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:04.320
20 laps in our fictional world we’ve&nbsp;
built. But we haven’t accounted for

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.560
something very important – other cars.
Sure, the model says you can do a lap

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:12.880
time of 3 time-blocks per lap, but not if you’re&nbsp;
stuck behind a car doing 4 time-blocks per lap.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.160
Every lap you’re stuck behind this joker&nbsp;
you’re adding a block of extra time to your

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:20.160
race and the strategy is going out the window.
So you have to be able to adapt. And that’s

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:24.800
where the plans B, C and D come on. You have&nbsp;
to imagine scenarios where you shift from the

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:30.400
paper-perfect strategy to something else.
In this case, if you can’t overtake,

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:35.600
what you’d ideally like is some clean air. Take&nbsp;
a pitstop and make hay with fresh tyres so go

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:40.480
as fast as you’re able to without being held up.
This is where strategists check where everyone is,

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:45.360
timing-wise. If this circle represents a full&nbsp;
lap – say 100 seconds – with all the cars

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:51.520
going around, and a pit delta is 20 seconds, then&nbsp;
taking a pit stop is going to drop you out here.

00:10:51.520 --> 00:10:55.840
At the moment, this is right behind three cars&nbsp;
who look to be in a fight. That’s not good news

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:00.400
and won’t allow you to push on new tyres. You&nbsp;
probably should wait for a better opportunity.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:04.560
A few laps later you’ve pulled a few seconds&nbsp;
over these squabbling cars and are predicted

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:08.880
to come out of the pits ahead of them by&nbsp;
this diagram. It’s time to pull the pin.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:12.000
When watching the race it’s good to know&nbsp;
the pit delta of the track we’re at so you

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.320
can look at the timing tower and work out where&nbsp;
someone will fall back to if they stop. In this

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:21.680
example we can look at, say, Leclerc and say if&nbsp;
the pit delta is 20 seconds, he’ll drop back a

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:28.240
few seconds behind Verstappen as 15 plus 20 is 35&nbsp;
and Verstappen is 32 seconds behind the leader.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:31.040
This is why I prefer the Leader&nbsp;
timings to the Interval ones as it’s

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:34.400
easier to do quick maths like that.
So, yeah you might have to react quickly

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.480
and adapt your strategy on the fly if you can’t&nbsp;
progress beyond the car in front, if the tyres

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:42.240
are behaving differently than expected or&nbsp;
if you’re holding out for a Safety Car.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:46.400
Taking a pitstop under a Safety Car can be&nbsp;
crucial as you can save so much time and

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:50.960
potentially jump several positions in the order.
The reason safety cars and virtual safety cars

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:54.640
offer such cheap pit stops comes back to&nbsp;
this little calculation we did before in

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.880
how long it takes to get from pit entry to&nbsp;
pit exit on track and through the pit roads.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:03.680
Let’s go back to Monaco again with our stopwatch.&nbsp;
We know at full speed the cars on track take 12.5

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:08.480
seconds to get from pit entry to pit exit and&nbsp;
32 to do the same journey through the pit lane.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:12.720
With the virtual safety car deployed that&nbsp;
same journey out on track takes about 18

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:17.120
seconds so you only lose 14 seconds instead&nbsp;
of 19.5 seconds. And with the full Safety

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:22.320
Car you could lose only 11 or 12 seconds.
Some tracks or situations (like rain) come

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.200
with a higher risk of safety car deployment than&nbsp;
others. If the risk is high, it might be worth

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.840
extending the stint you’re on in the hope that a&nbsp;
safety car is pulled and you can take advantage.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:35.840
How much time you decide to sacrifice by&nbsp;
continuing on degrading tyres is a matter

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:41.520
of risk/reward balance and somewhat calculated&nbsp;
live with telemetry from the tyres and feedback

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:46.080
on the driver on how they’re managing them.
And then we come to weather. We all know rain

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.920
makes the track slower and requires using&nbsp;
wet-weather tyres instead of slicks, but the

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:54.960
problem with rain is that it’s not consistent.
The strategy computer won’t just have one wet

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:58.800
track model because rain can be heavy or&nbsp;
light, it can affect different parts of

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.120
the track as it moves across the landscape.&nbsp;
Puddles can form and dry up in different ways

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.760
depending on track topology and how&nbsp;
warm it is. It’s a complicated mess.

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:11.360
You’ve also got to account for how well your&nbsp;
driver can manage changeable conditions. If

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:15.040
they’ve got the skill to manage dry&nbsp;
tyres on a damp track, you might not

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:19.200
change tyres if it looks like it might be a&nbsp;
passing light shower. Save yourself a stop.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:24.320
If we were to be simple about things we might draw&nbsp;
a graph like this showing, let’s say, soft tyres,

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:29.280
intermediates and full wets as the track gets&nbsp;
increasingly wet. I stress again, this is not

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:33.600
data-driven, it’s a very simplified shape.
As you can see, the soft tyres are great

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:38.320
until it's slightly wet and then they just&nbsp;
become catastrophically slow very quickly.

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:42.560
The lower the line, the faster the laptime,&nbsp;
so we see the inters quickly take over as the

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:46.960
faster tyre as the standing water increases.&nbsp;
The reason they fail if it’s too dry is they

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:50.960
simply overheat from all the tread movement.
And if it gets really wet, the full wets become

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:54.960
the fastest tyre as they can clear the most water,&nbsp;
but – let’s be honest – the race will be stopped

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:59.280
before they get to use those tyres. It’s amazing&nbsp;
they keep bringing them to races at this point.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:03.440
And finally it’s worth taking a quick look at&nbsp;
a strategy offset. Two competing drivers may

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:07.680
be on vaguely similar strategies – say,&nbsp;
a soft, then a medium – but one may pull

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.760
the trigger a couple of laps early in the hope&nbsp;
of getting some quick times in on new rubber.

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:16.800
We saw this earlier in the video with trying&nbsp;
to slot into a gap of fresh air. The aim is

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:21.360
that over the laps before their rival pits,&nbsp;
the early stopping driver will be fast enough

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:26.640
to move into this pit delta so their rival&nbsp;
comes out behind them. This is an undercut.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:31.040
The later-stopping driver may end up behind their&nbsp;
rival through the stops but they are now on the

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:36.320
fresher, FASTER tyre. In theory, they could&nbsp;
get position back if they are able to overtake.

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:40.640
To really accentuate this pace difference, the&nbsp;
later stopping car (having realised they are

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:45.760
to be undercut) may extend their stint even&nbsp;
longer so that when they pit their tyres will

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:50.480
be MUCH faster than the early stopping rival.
The balance is they will have lost MORE time

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:55.200
by waiting to stop and they’ll now have to&nbsp;
try to catch and pass on the fresh rubber.

00:14:55.200 --> 00:15:00.080
This is overcutting and tends to be trickier,&nbsp;
especially on an overtaking-unfriendly track.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:04.000
So that’s your basics on visualising strategy. I&nbsp;
hope it gave something to put in your head when

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:27.120
watching races or, at the very least, you enjoyed&nbsp;
watching coloured squares move around. Good bye.

